<<<
dislocate. See -ATABLE.
===
**dislocate.** See ^^-ATABLE^^.
>>>

<<<
dislodg(e)ment. Keep the -e-; see JUDGEMENT.
===
**dislodg(e)ment.** Keep the *-e-*; see ^^JUDGEMENT^^.
>>>

<<<
dismal. For ‘the d. science’ see SOBRIQUETS, - For comparison, see -ER & -EST, 4.
===
**dismal.** For 'the d. science' see ^^SOBRIQUETS^^. For comparison, see ^^-ER^^ & ^^-EST^^, 4.
>>>

<<<
dismission, the predecessor of dismissal, has been completely dispossessed by it, & must now be regarded as a NEEDLESS VARIANT.
===
**dismission,** the predecessor of *dismissal*, has been completely dispossessed by it, & must now be regarded as a ^^NEEDLESS VARIANT^^.
>>>

<<<
dispatch, des-. The OED gives good reasons for preferring dis-. See also FORMAL WORDS.
===
**dispatch, des-.** The OED gives good reasons for preferring *dis-*. See also ^^FORMAL WORDS^^.
>>>

<<<
dispel means to drive away in different directions, & must have for object a word to which that sense is applicable (darkness, fear, cloud, suspicions), & not, as in the following sentence, a single indivisible thing:—
===
**dispel** means to drive away in different directions, & must have for object a word to which that sense is applicable (*darkness, fear, cloud, suspicions*), & not, as in the following sentence, a single indivisible thing:—
>>>

<<<
Lord Carrington effectually dispelled yesterday the suggestion that he resigned the Presidency because he feared . . . He might dispel
===
*Lord Carrington effectually dispelled yesterday the suggestion that he resigned the Presidency because he feared* . . . He might dispel
>>>

<<<
dispensable. For the sense that can be dispensed with, see -ABLE 4,
===
**dispensable.** For the sense *that can be dispensed with*, see ^^-ABLE^^ 4.
>>>

<<<
dispersedly. Four syllables; see “EDLY.
===
**dispersedly.** Four syllables; see ^^-EDLY^^.
>>>

<<<
dispiteous. See REVIVALS.
===
**dispiteous.** See ^^REVIVALS^^.
>>>

<<<
displeasedly. A bad form; see -EDLY.
===
**displeasedly.** A bad form; see ^^-EDLY^^.
>>>

<<<
disposable. For the sense that can be disposed of, see -ABLE 4,
===
**disposable.** For the sense *that can be disposed of*, see ^^-ABLE^^ 4.
>>>

<<<
disposal, disposition. In some contexts there is no choice (His -ition is merciful; The -al of the empty bottles is a difficulty); in some either word may be used indifferently (Zhe money is at your -al or -ition);
===
**disposal, disposition.** In some contexts there is no choice (*His -ition is merciful*; *The -al of the empty bottles is a difficulty*); in some either word may be used indifferently (*The money is at your -al or -ition*);
>>>

<<<
& in some the choice depends upon the sense required (The -ition of the troops is the way they are stationed for action &c., & is general’s work; The -al of the troops is the way they are lodged &c. when not being used, & is quartermaster’s work).
===
& in some the choice depends upon the sense required (*The -ition of the troops is the way they are stationed for action &c., & is general's work*; *The -al of the troops is the way they are lodged &c. when not being used, & is quartermaster's work*).
>>>

<<<
When doubt arises, it is worth while to remember that -ition corresponds to dispose, & -al to dispose of. So The -ition of the books is excellent (they are excellently disposed, i.e. arranged), but The -al of the books
===
When doubt arises, it is worth while to remember that *-ition* corresponds to *dispose*, & *-al* to *dispose of*. So *The -ition of the books is excellent* (they are excellently disposed, i.e. arranged), but *The -al of the books*
>>>

<<<
+ was soon managed (they were soon disposed of, i.e. either sold or got out of the way); The -ition of the body is stiff (it is stiffly disposed, i.c. arranged), but The -al of the body proved impossible (it could not be disposed of, i.e. destroyed or concealed),
===
+ *was soon managed* (they were soon disposed of, i.e. either sold or got out of the way); *The -ition of the body is stiff* (it is stiffly disposed, i.e. arranged), but *The -al of the body proved impossible* (it could not be disposed of, i.e. destroyed or concealed),
>>>

<<<
The testamentary -ition of property, i.e. the way it is disposed or arranged by will, & The testamentary -al of properiy, i.e. the way it is disposed of or transferred by will, describing the same act from different points of view, are naturally used without much discrimination.
===
*The testamentary -ition of property*, i.e. the way it is disposed or arranged by will, & *The testamentary -al of property*, i.e. the way it is disposed of or transferred by will, describing the same act from different points of view, are naturally used without much discrimination.
>>>

<<<
The same is true of at one’s -al or -ition; but in this formula -al is now much commoner, just as You may dispose of the money as you please is now commoner than You may dispose it.
===
The same is true of *at* one's *-al* or *-ition*; but in this formula *-al* is now much commoner, just as *You may dispose of the money as you please* is now commoner than *You may dispose it*.
>>>

<<<
disproved, -en. The first is recommended; see PROVE.
===
**disproved, -en.** The first is recommended; see ^^PROVE^^.
>>>

<<<
disputable. Accent di’spiitabl, not dispt’tabl; see RECESSIVE ACCENT.
===
**disputable.** Accent dĭ'spūtabl, not dĭspū'tabl; see ^^RECESSIVE ACCENT^^.
>>>

<<<
dissatisfiedly. A bad form; see -EDLY.
===
**dissatisfiedly.** A bad form; see ^^-EDLY^^.
>>>

<<<
disseise, -ze, disseisin, -zin.

Spell -se, -SİN; See SEIZE.
===
**disseise, -ze, disseisin, -zin.** Spell *-se, -sin*; see ^^SEIZE^^.
>>>

<<<
dissemble, dissimulate. There is no clear line of distinction between the two. Dissemble is the word in ordinary use, & the other might have perished as a NEEDLESS VARIANT,
===
**dissemble, dissimulate.** There is no clear line of distinction between the two. *Dissemble* is the word in ordinary use, & the other might have perished as a ^^NEEDLESS VARIANT^^,
>>>

<<<
but has perhaps been kept in being because it is, unlike dissemble, provided with a noun (dissimulation), & a contrasted verb (simulate), & is more convenient for use in connexion with these.
===
but has perhaps been kept in being because it is, unlike *dissemble*, provided with a noun (*dissimulation*), & a contrasted verb (*simulate*), & is more convenient for use in connexion with these.
>>>

<<<
disseminate makes -nable, -tor; see -ABLE 1, -OR.
===
**disseminate** makes *-nable, -tor*; see ^^-ABLE^^ 1, ^^-OR^^.
>>>

<<<
dissimilation. See TECHNICAL TERMS.
===
**dissimilation.** See ^^TECHNICAL TERMS^^.
>>>

